{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Root cause confirmed by controlled single-variable experiment.\n\n**The cause is an ambient command-line Git configuration that leaves no usable credential source, which rebar then inherits verbatim.** The failing shape is `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=8` carrying `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null`, **no** `http.https://github.com/.extraheader`, and a **blank `credential.helper=`** entry. Because `GIT_CONFIG_*` is applied with command-line precedence - last, after system, global and repo config - the blank entry resets the helper list and thereby clears the repository's own `credential.helper = !gh auth git-credential`. Git is then left with no helper, no extraheader, and no terminal, and exits 128 with `fatal: unable to get password from user`. rebar copies that environment at `src/rebar/_store/push.py:242` and invokes the direct push at `push.py:250`.\n\n**The decisive measurement:** removing only the blank `credential.helper=` override, while keeping `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null` and everything else identical, makes the same push succeed through the repo-local `!gh auth git-credential` helper. One variable, both directions.\n\n**Why the earlier probe on this ticket was misleading, and the lesson for anyone reading back.** The by-hand pushes recorded above as \"succeeds with the ambient configuration intact\" were not run under the same effective configuration as the failures. That shell carried the auth-bearing 30-entry shape, which includes the GitHub extraheader; the failing invocations carry the 8-entry shape, which does not. `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT` alone is not the discriminator and I treated it as one. The credential-relevant keys I printed were filtered to `credential.*` and helper patterns, so an extraheader present in that environment would not have appeared in the output I reasoned from. The configuration varies between invocations, so a single observation of it is not evidence about another invocation.\n\n**What that does and does not revise.** The retraction stands on its own terms: rebar performs no environment scrub, and `push_env = {**os.environ, \"PRE_COMMIT_ALLOW_NO_CONFIG\": \"1\"}` plus an unmodified `run_git` forward is exactly what the code does. What was wrong in the original filing was the mechanism - inheritance of a credential-hostile config, not removal of a credential-bearing one. The observation that a blank `credential.helper` is implicated was right; the story about how it got that way was not.\n\n**Hypotheses eliminated, each by measurement:**\n\n- **H1, rebar mutates the environment: eliminated.** Instrumenting the `_git` seam showed no relevant difference between `os.environ` and the `env` handed to the push.\n- **H2, `capture_output=True` denies git a usable stdio channel: eliminated.** Captured and inherited stdio fail identically under the 8-entry shape, and a captured Python subprocess succeeds under the auth-bearing shape. Stdio is not the variable.\n- **H3, the non-fast-forward recovery path invokes git differently: eliminated.** The trace shows the failing call is the direct `git push origin HEAD:tickets` at `push.py:250`; no recovery fetch or merge ran.\n\n**Sentinel relationship.** At the time of measurement the tracker was `ahead=1`, `behind=0`, with `origin/tickets` an ancestor of `HEAD`. So the `push-pending` sentinel here is a straightforward consequence of the failed publication. It is **not** the no-common-ancestor `stale-store: diverged` condition; that remains independent and tracked on `abnormal-dear-shoveler` (`16c9-a42d-fd01-48b9`).\n\n**Fix direction is NOT yet settled, and deliberately so.** The narrowest technical fix is to construct a push-specific environment that preserves ambient Git settings but drops the blank `credential.helper=` override when no auth extraheader is present, letting the repo-local helper run. That is a fix, but it has rebar overriding what may be a deliberate operator security posture: a blank credential helper is a legitimate way to say \"this process may not acquire credentials.\" Silently reversing that for rebar's own housekeeping push is a design decision, not an implementation detail, and it needs an explicit choice between overriding the ambient posture, requiring an explicitly configured credential source, and failing loudly but diagnosably instead of silently. That choice is being escalated rather than assumed.\n\nAC5 should be read in light of this: the push path has no hermeticity property today, so a test asserting one would be vacuous. What it does have is an inheritance property, and that is what needs stating and testing."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789746140641278628,"uuid":"cbcf7029-de98-4cfb-8c8e-19c7d0f42b55"}